      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense, \"stochastic jitter\" sounds like the right direction for the paper. \n\nI'm also looking at the /z/ sound for comparison. When the voicing is active, the noise doesn't feel as \"skittering\" as the /s/; instead, it has this much heavier, more rhythmic, almost \"gnawing\" or \"munching\" quality to the texture of the sound. It feels much less erratic and more purposeful, like it's constantly working through the air.\n\nIs there a way to describe that specific sense of a \"heavy\" or \"burrowing\" texture in the way the periodic voicing interacts with the aperiodic noise? I want to capture that \"busy\" or \"active\" feeling of the sound without drifting into non-technical language.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The sensation you're describing for the /z/ phoneme, where the voicing imparts a \"heavy\" or \"burrowing\" texture, can be characterized as \"voiced sibilant noise.\" In this context, the voiced sibilant noise refers to the periodic vibration of the vocal folds that modulates the aperiodic noise of the /z/ sound. This modulation creates a \"busy\" or \"active\" acoustic signal, as opposed to the \"skittering\" quality of the voiceless /s/. The voiced sibilant noise can be thought of as the \"munching\" or \"gnawing\" texture you mentioned, which is a result of the periodic excitation of the vocal folds interacting with the aperiodic noise. In your dissertation, you might want to explore the acoustic properties of this voiced sibilant noise, such as its fundamental frequency, amplitude, and spectral characteristics, and how they contribute to the overall perception of the /z/ sound.",
      "source": "target",
